<%# ONE GAME, ON ONE LINE. Locals: game (Game).

    Reads as a scoreboard line — `17 JAX  LAC 28` — with the SCORES on the
    outside edges and the two teams meeting in the middle. That is the shape a
    reader already knows from a TV bug or a ticker, so a strip of sixteen of
    them scans without being studied.

    THE TWO TEAMS BLEND INTO EACH OTHER. One gradient runs the width of the
    chip, each team's own field holding its end and the pair meeting in the
    middle, so the chip reads as one object rather than two cards stuck
    together. The halves are still separate elements — the score animations need
    a per-team box — but they are transparent and only the gradient beneath is
    seen. Nothing divides them: no slash, no rule, no rail. At 168px every mark
    is competing with the four characters that carry the meaning.

    The strip's job is CHOOSING, not watching, so everything the tile below says
    better is gone: no city, no mascot, no scoring plays, no emoji.

    IT WEARS THE SAME ANIMATION HOOKS AS live/_game_tile — data-game-slug,
    data-team-slug, data-role="score", .nfl-row-wash, .nfl-sweep — on purpose. A
    score lands once and lights BOTH the chip and the focus tile, because the
    page's script queries every match rather than the first. If you add a hook
    there, add it here. (.nfl-rail is the exception: the rail it animates is the
    pipe that was removed, so there is nothing here for it to pulse.) %>
<% home = game.home_team %>
<% away = game.away_team %>
<% away_palette = team_card_palette(away) %>
<% home_palette = team_card_palette(home) %>
<% away_field = normalize_hex(away&.card_background) || "#3a3f4b" %>
<% home_field = normalize_hex(home&.card_background) || "#3a3f4b" %>
<button type="button"
        data-game-slug="<%= game.slug %>"
        data-test="live-game-chip"
        @click="focus = '<%= game.slug %>'"
        :class="focus === '<%= game.slug %>' ? 'tt-chip-focused' : ''"
        <%# THE SELECTED CHIP WEARS THE GLOW RING — the engine's .studio-team-glow,
            lit in this game's own two colours, which is a far better answer to
            "which one am I watching" than a border in a colour belonging to
            nobody. Opacity is driven by .tt-chip / .tt-chip-focused rather than
            set here, so the existing focus toggle turns it on and off for free.
            NOT overflow-hidden: the ring paints at a NEGATIVE inset, outside the
            host's box, and clipping the host clips the whole effect away. %>
        class="tt-chip card p-0 w-[168px] text-left transition studio-team-glow"
        style="--studio-team-glow-color: <%= away_palette[:accent] %>;
               --studio-team-glow-color-b: <%= home_palette[:accent] %>;
               --studio-team-glow-thickness: 2.5px;
               --studio-team-glow-bloom: 16px;
               --studio-team-glow-speed: 5s"
        :aria-pressed="focus === '<%= game.slug %>' ? 'true' : 'false'"
        aria-label="<%= away&.short_name || game.away_team_slug %> <%= game.away_score.to_i %>, <%= home&.short_name || game.home_team_slug %> <%= game.home_score.to_i %>">
  <div class="flex items-stretch" style="overflow: hidden; border-top-left-radius: inherit; border-top-right-radius: inherit;
       background: linear-gradient(100deg,
              <%= away_field %> 0%, <%= away_field %> 34%,
              <%= home_field %> 66%, <%= home_field %> 100%)">
    <% [[away, game.away_score, away_palette, :away], [home, game.home_score, home_palette, :home]].each do |team, score, palette, side| %>
      <%# The two halves MIRROR each other: the away score sits hard left, the
          home score hard right, and the abbreviations meet in the middle. %>
      <div class="relative flex-1 min-w-0 flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2 py-1.5 overflow-hidden<%= " flex-row-reverse" if side == :home %>"
           data-team-slug="<%= team&.slug %>"
           data-role="team-row"
           style="--nfl-team: <%= side == :away ? away_field : home_field %>;
                  --nfl-team-ink: <%= palette[:accent] %>;">
        <span class="nfl-row-wash absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none opacity-0"
              style="background: <%= palette[:accent] %>"></span>
        <span class="nfl-sweep absolute inset-y-0 left-0 pointer-events-none opacity-0"
              style="width: 45%;
                     background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, <%= palette[:accent] %>, transparent)"></span>
        <%# No inline colour on the score — an inline style beats a stylesheet
            rule, and the hot state could then never repaint it. %>
        <span class="relative text-sm font-extrabold tabular-nums flex-shrink-0"
              style="--nfl-team-fg: <%= palette[:fg] %>"
              data-role="score"><%= score.to_i %></span>
        <span class="relative min-w-0 flex-1 text-[11px] font-extrabold truncate<%= " text-right" if side == :home %>"
              style="color: <%= palette[:accent] %>; text-shadow: <%= palette[:mascot_shadow] %>">
          <%= team&.short_name || team&.mascot || team&.name %>
        </span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# ONE FACT, not two. The tile below shows the badge AND the feed's own
      detail because it has the width for both; at this size a scheduled game
      printed "FRI 11:00 PM" over "8/28 - 7:00 PM EDT" and wrapped. So the
      detail is kept only where it says something the badge cannot — the game
      clock on a live game — and a scheduled game shows its kickoff alone. %>
  <div class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-surface-alt/60 flex items-center justify-between gap-1 whitespace-nowrap"
       style="overflow: hidden; border-bottom-left-radius: inherit; border-bottom-right-radius: inherit">
    <% if game.live? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-[9px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-400 flex-shrink-0">
        <span class="h-1 w-1 rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse"></span> Live
      </span>
      <span class="text-[9px] text-secondary font-mono truncate"><%= game.status_detail.presence %></span>
    <% elsif game.completed? %>
      <span class="text-[9px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Final</span>
    <% elsif game.kickoff_at %>
      <%# Machine-readable, formatted by the BROWSER in the reader's own zone —
          the app runs on UTC, so a server-rendered kickoff is wrong for
          everyone. The text is a truthful no-JS fallback. %>
      <time datetime="<%= game.kickoff_at.utc.iso8601 %>"
            data-role="kickoff"
            class="text-[9px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted"><%= game.kickoff_at.utc.strftime("%a %-I:%M %p") %></time>
    <% else %>
      <span class="text-[9px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">TBD</span>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</button>
